{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/ramen-history-flavours-and-change/audio/main-6d1f952a213f.mp3","durationSec":304.68,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.54,"text":"Ramen has Chinese roots, but historians still debate exactly how it entered Japan."},{"start":5.62,"end":8.12,"text":"We should not imagine one simple arrival story."},{"start":8.74,"end":14.44,"text":"We do know that a Chinese restaurant in Asakusa was serving an early form of the dish by 1910."},{"start":15.66,"end":20.02,"text":"In Japan, cooks gradually adapted the soup, noodles, and toppings."},{"start":20.74,"end":25.62,"text":"The wheat noodles often contain kansui, which helps give them a firm, bouncy texture."},{"start":26.36,"end":31.28,"text":"We can understand this history through three ideas: source, change, and result."},{"start":32.4,"end":34.08,"text":"The source was Chinese noodle cooking."},{"start":35,"end":37.66,"text":"The change happened as Japanese cooks developed the dish."},{"start":38.36,"end":41.3,"text":"The result was ramen with many local and restaurant styles."},{"start":42.38,"end":45.04,"text":"Ramen became much more common after the Second World War."},{"start":46.12,"end":50.1,"text":"In 1945, people sold it in postwar black markets."},{"start":50.1,"end":57.04,"text":"Around 1950, more flour was available, and people returning to Japan also helped ramen shops and varieties expand."},{"start":57.96,"end":62.28,"text":"Ramen suited a changing society because a shop could prepare a filling bowl and serve it quickly."},{"start":63.04,"end":67.7,"text":"At the same time, different cooks could make different stocks, seasonings, noodles, and toppings."},{"start":68.76,"end":71.66,"text":"This helped ramen grow without becoming one fixed recipe."},{"start":72.66,"end":76.4,"text":"Using our framework, postwar life was an important source of change."},{"start":76.9,"end":80.62,"text":"The result was more shops and more opportunities for local styles to develop."},{"start":81.8,"end":89.12,"text":"Four broad ramen families became especially well known: shoyu, shio, miso, and tonkotsu."},{"start":89.12,"end":91.18,"text":"These names do not describe every ingredient."},{"start":91.72,"end":94.94,"text":"A stock may use chicken, pork bones, seafood, or a mixture."},{"start":95.56,"end":97.18,"text":"The cook then adds a main seasoning."},{"start":98.58,"end":103.06,"text":"Shoyu ramen uses soy sauce as its main seasoning, while shio ramen uses salt."},{"start":103.06,"end":108.92,"text":"However, two shoyu bowls can taste very different because their stocks, noodles, and toppings may differ."},{"start":109.66,"end":112.34,"text":"This balance helps explain the wide appeal of these families."},{"start":112.34,"end":116.74,"text":"Customers can recognize the main style, but cooks still have room to create."},{"start":117.54,"end":121.38,"text":"The name gives a clear identity, while the full bowl offers variety."},{"start":122.66,"end":127.24,"text":"Sapporo is strongly connected with miso ramen, which developed there in the 1950s."},{"start":127.7,"end":130.44,"text":"Its success was not only about adding miso to soup."},{"start":131.14,"end":133.84,"text":"The different parts of the bowl were designed to work together."},{"start":134.82,"end":139.52,"text":"Classic Sapporo miso ramen uses medium-thick, elastic, crinkled noodles."},{"start":140.22,"end":142.3,"text":"Their shape helps them hold the miso broth."},{"start":143.16,"end":145.84,"text":"Vegetables such as bean sprouts and onion are also common."},{"start":146.34,"end":150.34,"text":"The rich seasoning, noodle texture, and vegetables create a memorable combination."},{"start":151.04,"end":158.08,"text":"This shows why a flavour family can become widely loved: people experience a complete bowl, not only one seasoning."},{"start":158.68,"end":165.26,"text":"Here, the source is a local style, the change is careful design, and the result is a strong regional identity."},{"start":166.74,"end":169.3,"text":"Tonkotsu ramen is built around pork-bone broth."},{"start":170.04,"end":175.58,"text":"In 1947, cloudy tonkotsu broth was served in Kurume, in Fukuoka Prefecture."},{"start":176.44,"end":179.94,"text":"This became an important influence on later ramen across Kyushu."},{"start":180.86,"end":183.4,"text":"Hakata ramen is another famous Kyushu example."},{"start":183.98,"end":187.64,"text":"It usually has cloudy pork-bone broth and simple toppings."},{"start":188.08,"end":193.2,"text":"Customers can also order kaedama, an extra serving of noodles for the remaining soup."},{"start":193.76,"end":196.12,"text":"This gives diners some control over the meal."},{"start":196.94,"end":205.24,"text":"Tonkotsu became memorable because its broth gave it a clear identity, while shop practices such as noodle refills made the experience distinctive."},{"start":205.8,"end":210.76,"text":"Again, one regional change produced results that travelled beyond its original city."},{"start":212.04,"end":213.76,"text":"Ramen kept changing as it spread."},{"start":214.38,"end":220.02,"text":"Each restaurant could choose its stock, seasoning, noodle thickness, toppings, and local ingredients."},{"start":220.98,"end":224.72,"text":"These choices helped create today’s regional and shop-level variety."},{"start":225.92,"end":230.78,"text":"Cooks also made mixed broths, tsukemen with separate dipping soup, and soupless styles."},{"start":231.5,"end":233.46,"text":"Ramen received wider attention overseas."},{"start":233.46,"end":236.7,"text":"Restaurant ramen therefore changed in two directions."},{"start":237.44,"end":242.44,"text":"Some cooks protected a local identity, while others combined ideas and created something new."},{"start":243.31,"end":252.82,"text":"In Japan, a bowl connected with a particular city can feel different from a convenient bowl near a station or workplace, even when both belong to the same broad flavour family."},{"start":254.08,"end":258.62,"text":"Restaurant ramen depends on a cook, a kitchen, and a bowl served soon after preparation."},{"start":260.28,"end":262.16,"text":"Instant noodles changed this relationship."},{"start":263.18,"end":270.66,"text":"In 1958, Momofuku Ando invented Chicken Ramen, which Nissin describes as the world’s first instant noodles."},{"start":271.6,"end":277.46,"text":"In 1971, he developed Cup Noodles, described by Nissin as the first cup-type instant noodles."},{"start":278.22,"end":282.32,"text":"These products made noodles much easier to store, prepare, and distribute."},{"start":282.32,"end":288.38,"text":"Ramen-style food could now be eaten at home, at work, or in other places without a ramen shop."},{"start":289.22,"end":292.4,"text":"Instant noodles did not replace regional restaurant bowls."},{"start":293.02,"end":299.16,"text":"Instead, they created another path: less individual shop design, but much greater convenience and reach."},{"start":299.16,"end":304.18,"text":"The result was a food connected both with local craft and everyday speed."}],"words":[{"text":"Ramen","start":0,"end":0.36},{"text":"has","start":0.36,"end":0.54},{"text":"Chinese","start":0.54,"end":0.9},{"text":"roots","start":0.9,"end":1.28},{"text":"but","start":1.86,"end":1.94},{"text":"historians","start":1.94,"end":2.38},{"text":"still","start":2.38,"end":2.7},{"text":"debate","start":2.7,"end":2.98},{"text":"exactly","start":2.98,"end":3.38},{"text":"how","start":3.38,"end":3.72},{"text":"it","start":3.72,"end":3.84},{"text":"entered","start":3.84,"end":4.12},{"text":"Japan","start":4.12,"end":4.54},{"text":"We","start":5.62,"end":5.76},{"text":"should","start":5.76,"end":5.9},{"text":"not","start":5.9,"end":6.12},{"text":"imagine","start":6.12,"end":6.5},{"text":"one","start":6.5,"end":6.82},{"text":"simple","start":6.82,"end":7.24},{"text":"arrival","start":7.24,"end":7.52},{"text":"story","start":7.52,"end":8.12},{"text":"We","start":8.74,"end":8.9},{"text":"do","